# Session 7 — the key for a copy that is OVER: nothing is started, nothing is asked of a guest.
#
#   mrn-verify --refresh=never key-recorded.mrv
#
# `--refresh=never' means: do not ask any guest for a fresh report, read the last one it wrote.
# On a copy whose machines were shut down properly, that report is inside the project, and so
# are the four archived documents. This is how thirty copies are marked in one evening — but
# read what each assertion rests on:
#
#   · `documents' is host-written for the console and the terminal recording, guest-written for
#     the report and the history. A FAIL on a guest-written document is a finding, not a
#     sanction: quote the terminal recording next to it;
#   · nothing here proves connectivity. An accessibility is an experiment, and an experiment
#     needs the lab to run — that is what key.mrv is for.

# the model survived the session
field r1 port_no is 3
cable c2 r1:eth0 h1:port2
cable c3 r1:eth2 h3:port1

# the session was closed the way the statement asked: properly, machine by machine
state m1 is off
state r1 is off

# what the shutdown archived — the terminal recording is the piece a mark may rest on
# Two measured details shape these three lines:
#   · the titles of archived documents are TRANSLATED (a French session files a report under
#     « Rapport sur r1 »), so a pattern that assumes English marks nothing abroad — hence the
#     alternative below. Nothing in the channel is localised; this treeview is;
#   · a report is archived only if it EXISTS when the machine shuts down. Ask each guest for one
#     during the session (`report <c>') if you want to be sure: see § 5.5 of the teacher's guide.
documents m1 has ~ Terminal of
documents r1 has ~ Terminal of
documents r1 has ~ (Report on|Rapport sur)

# what the last report of r1 said about itself
report r1 says ~ ip_forward *= *1
report r1 says ~ ^-A POSTROUTING .*MASQUERADE
